ABOUT MONTYSOLR
===============

MontySolr is the search engine behind Astrophysics Data System (ADS 2.0) - 
"the world's most complete collection of scholarly content in the physical sciences" - 
http://ads.harvard.edu/. It is built on top of SOLR (http://lucene.apache.org/solr) 
but it is not a fork. New features are added as extensions. You can use MontySolr's 
infrastructure to add new functionality or test patches without duplicating code.

So what is here:

  - extremely powerful query parser (equivalent of which you won't find anywhere else)
  - second order queries (operators): analytical query operations
  - batch processing for long-running tasks
  - bitset queries (to search/analyze millions of docs by sending their IDs)
  - author name disambiguation
  - extensive tokenization rules
  - multi-synonym parsing and expansion
  - Java author-name parsing
  - and of course: the whole configuration of the ADS search engine
  
  
Author parsing runs in Java. The legacy JythonNameParser and
PythonicAuthorNormalizerFilter names remain in the Java API and configuration;
they do not require a Python or Jython runtime.
  


BUILDING FROM SOURCE
====================

Install JDK 21 and use the checked-in Gradle wrapper (no separate Gradle
installation is required). The build targets Solr 9.6.1 and Lucene 9.10.0.
Run these commands from the repository root:

```bash
git clone https://github.com/adsabs/montysolr
cd montysolr
./gradlew clean build
```

The build generates the ANTLR parsers, compiles the Java extensions, runs the
current Java tests, and stages deployment dependencies. Its main outputs are:

  - montysolr/build/libs/montysolr.jar: the Java extension jar
  - deploy/build/libs/: the extension jar and its runtime dependency jars
  - montysolr/build/reports/tests/test/index.html: the Java test report

Useful individual tasks:

```bash
./gradlew :montysolr:test
./gradlew :montysolr:generateGrammarSource
./gradlew :deploy:copyDependenciesToLib
```

Generated parser sources and token maps live under
montysolr/build/generated-src/antlr/main; edit the grammar sources rather than
generated output. For IDE development, import the root project as a Gradle
project, select the wrapper and JDK 21, and let Gradle configure the source sets
and dependencies instead of using checked-in Eclipse or PyDev metadata.


DEPLOYMENT
==========

deploy/adsabs is the retained ADS configuration overlay, not a standalone Solr
installation. It supplies the collection configuration and dictionaries, startup
profiles, logging and Jetty configuration, and standalone JMeter assets.

Provision a compatible Solr installation separately, then integrate the overlay
with that installation. Make the extension and required runtime jars staged in
deploy/build/libs available on Solr's library path, accounting for dependencies
already supplied by Solr. Review the collection's library paths and the startup
profile's index, batch, log and port settings for your environment before
starting Solr with that installation's launcher.

Install the overlay's conf/core.properties in the core directory itself
(collection1/core.properties), not under collection1/conf; leaving it under conf
can cause duplicate core discovery. The deployed configuration also requires
Solr's scripting module for XSLT support. Provide and enable that module in the
Solr installation separately: it is a test-only dependency here and is not
included in the staged runtime jars.

Gradle stages jars only: it does not assemble a complete Solr distribution,
install the overlay, or start a configured server. The source checkout cannot be
started as a Solr distribution directly.


RETIRED TOOLING
===============

The Ant/Ivy build and distribution assembler, Python/Jython implementation and
packaged scripts, and Python deployment, reporting and debugging tools have
been retired. Java author parsing, the current Gradle Java test suites, and the
standalone Java grammar and JMeter tools remain supported.

The dormant Ant deployment-test harness was also retired. It was outside the
Gradle test source sets and contained assembled-deployment and pivot assertions;
the current Gradle tests are not claimed to replace that coverage. Deployment
assembly and the removed operational tools have no replacement in this build.
